    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d426">

                <pb facs="0610" n="486" sameAs="frus:frus1923v02/pg486"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">894.48B/207</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Japanese Ambassador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Hanihara</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1923-10-18"><hi rendition="#italic">October 18,
                        1923</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 78</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Recurring to my response of 4th
                        October<anchor n="33" xml:id="d426-f1"/> to your note of 29th September, I
                    have the honor to inform you that I am now instructed by His Majesty’s Minister
                    of State for Foreign Affairs to express to you once more, on behalf of my
                    Government and people, their profound appreciation of the wholehearted and
                    effective aid given to Japan by the Government and people of the United States
                    as well as by their Red Cross and other institutions.</p>
                <p>I am instructed also to thank you sincerely for your most sympathetic offer of
                    still further assistance that may be desired by my Government, and to inform you
                    that, in so far as the emergency relief personnel and supplies are concerned,
                    the needs of the moment are now being happily met, thanks to the prompt and
                    practical <pb facs="0611" n="487" xml:id="pg487"/> generosity of America and
                    other countries, and therefore that my Government wish to make no further call
                    upon your kindness in these respects.</p>
                <p>As regards the assistance that may be needed by my Government in the prosecution
                    of the reconstruction work, they hope they may count upon the good will of the
                    United States Government in so far as such assistance is within its power to
                    give.</p>
                <p>In sending you this communication, I avail myself [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">M. Hanihara</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
